{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-31e1-7587-ae09-a7cb3cfe4d49","id":"01a1a65f-aebc-7234-ad6b-f5fe9ba44824","idempotency_key":"32d99601dd2be58c28c18f5ed968245737f94280aa313498bfb325aaf6633bd6","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T11:44:10Z","payload":{"note":"Closed 5/5. Publishing already froze a schema rbc-study/1 bundle (study + chapters + their boards) and that part now has a test proving a later chapter cannot change it. New: schema v11 adds studies.locked, a manual do-not-change that is INDEPENDENT of publishing - a draft can be locked, and publishing does not make a lock redundant because unlocking a published study still refuses edits for the other reason. branch_study_from_version copies a published version's chapters into a new draft and records branched_from {study_id, version} in metadata, so lineage is a fact in the data rather than a convention; boards are shared rather than cloned, because a branch that duplicated every position would double the database on every edit. Routes: POST /api/studies/{id}/lock, POST /branch (defaults to the latest version), GET /versions. Two of my expectations were wrong and the tests corrected them: add_chapter bumps current_version, so a fresh study sits at 1 and not 0, and the lock test belonged in rbc-ai rather than rbc-db because the repository only stores the lock while the tools enforce it - rbc-db cannot depend on rbc-ai, which is the dependency direction the whole crate split rests on.","target":"t-376e"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
